# FigDown — figures as text. Spec: https://github.com/FigDown/figdown

# Genre: statechart — EXPERIMENTAL, and it needs `figdown 0.2` (`STATECHART-GENRE-SCOPE`). The two
# companion TABLE sections below stay `figdown 0.1`: the version a section
# declares is the surface that section needs, and a table needs nothing from
# 0.2. Sections are separate documents (`MULTI-FIGURE-DOCUMENTS`), so the versions may differ.
figdown 0.2 statechart
title "BFD Session State Machine (RFC 5880 §6.2)"

# ── What this figure is ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# The BFD session state machine transcribed from RFC 5880 (Katz & Ward,
# June 2010) — from the RFC ITSELF, §6.2's own ASCII diagram plus the
# normative prose, not from any vendor restatement of it.
#
# WHY THAT PROVENANCE IS WORTH A COMMENT. Downstream transcriptions of
# this machine circulate with THREE states (Down, Init, Up), treating
# AdminDown only as a value that can arrive in a received packet.
# RFC 5880 §6.2 is explicit that there are FOUR: "There are three states
# through which a session normally proceeds: two for establishing a
# session (Init and Up) and one for tearing down a session (Down) …
# A fourth state (AdminDown) exists so that a session can be
# administratively put down indefinitely." AdminDown is BOTH a local
# session state and a value carried in the State (Sta) field — §4.1
# encodes Sta as 0=AdminDown, 1=Down, 2=Init, 3=Up — and conflating the
# two is exactly the error this figure exists to not repeat.
#
# WHICH TEXT GOVERNS. §6.2's diagram is a summary and says so: it notes
# "Transitions involving AdminDown state are deleted for clarity (but
# are fully specified in sections 6.8.6 and 6.8.16)." So the diagram is
# transcribed here for its three states' arcs, and §6.8.6 (Reception of
# BFD Control Packets), §6.8.4/§6.8.5 (detection-time and Echo failure)
# and §6.8.16 (Administrative Control) supply the rest. Where the two
# disagree, the prose wins; see the DOWN self-loop note below, which is
# the one place they do.
#
# Notation on every transition label follows the RFC's own: the arc
# names the state of the REMOTE system as received in the State field,
# or the expiry of the Detection Timer. `rx X` = a BFD Control packet
# arrived carrying Sta = X.

# HONEST LIMIT (guide/expressing.md known limits — "closed-world readings"; the
# genre's own reading rule 5, spec/genres/experimental/statechart.md):
# the transition function is TOTAL and this figure cannot say so.
# RFC 5880 §6.8.6 defines an outcome for EVERY (local state, received
# Sta) pair — sixteen of them, plus a defined outcome for the Detection
# Timer in every state. Nothing is left to the implementer. That
# TOTALITY is a fact about the machine, and it is the fact a decision
# table asserts structurally: every cell is filled, so a reader can see
# at a glance that nothing was forgotten.
#
# A transition graph with merged triggers cannot assert it. The
# statechart genre's reading rules say a reader may conclude that "the
# state set is closed as drawn", but adds in the same breath that
# "absence is still not prohibition — a transition not drawn is not
# stated to be forbidden". That is the gap, stated by the genre itself:
# there is NO construct — no keyword, no option key, no annotation —
# that lets an author claim "the pairs not drawn here are not missing;
# they are the pairs whose defined outcome is no state change". A
# reading agent therefore cannot distinguish a deliberately-omitted
# pair from a forgotten one, and neither can a human.
#
# What this figure does INSTEAD, and it is a workaround, not the
# construct: (a) it draws the three no-change self-loops the RFC's own
# diagram draws, plus the fourth (AdminDown) that the RFC omits for
# clarity, so every state visibly answers for the events that leave it
# unmoved; and (b) it carries the full 4×5 decision table as a
# companion `table` section below — composition, the same interim
# `tcp-handshake.fd` uses to carry lifeline state that `topology` has
# no construct for. The table asserts totality by being complete. The
# statechart above does not assert it at all; a reader gets it from the
# panel or from the prose, never from the model.

# ── Semantic classes — meaning first, colour is only render ──────────
class states  "A state IS a BFD session state (RFC 5880 §6.2); the local value of bfd.SessionState" fill=#eef2ff
class advance "Session-establishment transition — the three-way handshake climbing Down → Init → Up (RFC 5880 §6.8.6)" stroke=#16a34a
class fail    "Session teardown on failure — peer signalled Down/AdminDown, Detection Time expired, or the Echo function failed (RFC 5880 §6.8.4, §6.8.5, §6.8.6)" stroke=#dc2626
class admin   "Administrative control — an operator action from outside BFD, not a received packet (RFC 5880 §6.8.16)" stroke=#9333ea style=dashed
class hold    "No state change: the event has a defined outcome and that outcome is to stay put (RFC 5880 §6.8.6)" stroke=#64748b style=dotted

# ── The four states (RFC 5880 §6.2; Sta encoding from §4.1) ──────────
state down "DOWN\nSta=1" class=states
# Session is down, or has just been created. §6.2: it remains Down until
# the remote system agrees the session is down by sending State other
# than Up. Semantically the forwarding path is unavailable. A system MAY
# hold a session here indefinitely by simply refusing to advance.

state init "INIT\nSta=2" class=states
# The remote system is communicating and the local system wants the
# session up, but the remote system does not yet realise it. §6.2.

state up "UP\nSta=3" class=states
# The session is established; connectivity between the systems is
# working. §6.2.

state admindown "ADMINDOWN\nSta=0" class=states
# Held administratively down. §6.2: this causes the REMOTE system to
# enter Down and remain there until the local system leaves AdminDown,
# and it has no semantic implication for the availability of the
# forwarding path. §6.8.6 discards every received packet while here, so
# no arriving packet can move the session out — only §6.8.16 can.

# `down` rather than the genre default `right`: this machine climbs
# Down → Init → Up and falls back the same way, so the reading axis is
# the ladder. `flow right` was tried and rejected on the render — it
# packs all four states into one row and the merged trigger labels pile
# up on each other around INIT.
flow down

# NO LAYOUT ZONE. THAT WAS A FORCED CHOICE UNTIL 0.4; IT IS NOW
# ONLY A CHOICE, AND THE FIGURE HAS NOT BEEN RE-MADE YET.
# Every RFC-faithful FSM figure in this repository is hand-pinned, and
# this one could not be: until 0.4 the engine drew a
# self-transition as a small side loop ONLY when neither endpoint was
# pinned. Pinning any state degenerated its self-transition to a
# zero-length line at the state's centre, with the trigger label printed
# on top of the state's own name — all four self-loops vanished and all
# four state names were obliterated. That was built and looked at before
# this comment was first written, and since all four states carry a
# self-transition there was no partial pinning that escaped it.
# 0.4 fixed it (engine backlog 64): a self-transition now draws
# the same way whether its state's coordinate came from a pin or from
# the layout pass. This figure still takes the automatic layout and
# still ships with the render defects noted at the foot of this file —
# what changed is that the arrangement is now available to it, and
# choosing pins for this machine is a placement decision to be measured
# and made, not a door that is shut.

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# TRANSITIONS — triggers MERGED onto shared arcs
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# The point of the graph over the table: §6.8.6 has fewer distinct
# state changes than the table has cells. Of the totality table's
# twenty cells (four states × five events), NINE are state changes —
# and those nine collapse onto FIVE arcs, plus the four no-change
# self-loops that carry the other eleven cells, because several events
# share one outcome: "rx AdminDown", "Detect Time expired" and "Echo
# failed" all mean Up → Down, and the RFC writes them in three separate
# sections. The four ADMINISTRATIVE arcs below are not among those
# twenty cells at all — §6.8.16 sits outside the packet-reception rules
# and has its own table. Thirteen `transition` lines in total: 5 + 4
# state-changing, 4 self-loops.
#
# WHY "Echo failed" IS DRAWN ON TWO ARCS AND HAS NO COLUMN. It is
# §6.8.5, and the Echo function is not active in every state, so the RFC
# defines no outcome for it in every row. A totality table asserts
# totality by having every cell filled, and a column whose cells the RFC
# leaves undefined would break exactly the claim the table exists to
# make. So Echo failure rides on the teardown arcs, where §6.8.5 does
# define it, and the table stays the grid it can honestly fill.

# ── Climbing: the three-way handshake (§6.8.6) ──────────────────────
transition down -[rx Down]-> init class=advance
# §6.8.6: "If bfd.SessionState is Down / If received State is Down /
# Set bfd.SessionState to Init". The peer agrees the session is down.

transition down -[rx Init]-> up class=advance
# §6.8.6: "Else if received State is Init / Set bfd.SessionState to Up".
# The peer is already one step ahead, so Down goes straight to Up.

transition init -[rx Init, rx Up]-> up class=advance
# §6.8.6: "Else if bfd.SessionState is Init / If received State is Init
# or Up / Set bfd.SessionState to Up". Two triggers, one outcome —
# merged onto one arc rather than drawn twice. Matches the RFC diagram's
# own arc label "INIT, UP".

# ── Falling: teardown (§6.8.4, §6.8.5, §6.8.6) ──────────────────────
transition init -["rx AdminDown\nDetect expired, Echo failed"]-> down class=fail
# Three sources, one arc. §6.8.6: received AdminDown while
# bfd.SessionState is not Down sets LocalDiag 3 (Neighbor signaled
# session down) and the state to Down. §6.8.4: Detection Time passes
# without a packet while the state is Init or Up — LocalDiag 1 (Control
# Detection Time Expired). §6.8.5: Echo function failed — LocalDiag 2.

transition up -["rx Down, rx AdminDown\nDetect expired, Echo failed"]-> down class=fail
# The same three, plus received Down: §6.8.6 "Else (bfd.SessionState is
# Up) / If received State is Down / Set bfd.LocalDiag to 3 … Set
# bfd.SessionState to Down". Four triggers, one arc. The RFC diagram
# writes this arc "ADMIN DOWN, DOWN, TIMER"; Echo failure is §6.8.5,
# which the diagram does not cover at all.

# ── Administrative control (§6.8.16) — not a received packet ────────
transition down -[admin disable]-> admindown class=admin
transition init -[admin disable]-> admindown class=admin
transition up -[admin disable]-> admindown class=admin
# §6.8.16: "Else / Set bfd.SessionState to AdminDown / Set
# bfd.LocalDiag to an appropriate value / Cease the transmission of BFD
# Echo packets". Drawn from all three other states because the RFC puts
# no precondition on it. These are the arcs §6.2's diagram deletes for
# clarity, and the reason a three-state transcription looks complete.

transition admindown -[admin enable]-> down class=admin
# §6.8.16: "If enabling session / Set bfd.SessionState to Down". The
# ONLY way out of AdminDown — §6.8.6 discards every received packet
# while the session is AdminDown, so no peer can lift it.

# ── No-change self-loops: the defined outcome is "stay put" ─────────
transition down -["rx Up, rx AdminDown\nDetect expired"]-> down class=hold
# The RFC diagram draws this self-loop and labels it exactly
# "UP, ADMIN DOWN, TIMER".
#
# WHERE THE DIAGRAM AND THE PROSE DISAGREE, AND THE PROSE WINS. The
# diagram's self-loop suggests an event with an effect. §6.8.6 says
# otherwise for all three: received Up while Down falls through every
# branch (Down handles only received Down and received Init), received
# AdminDown is guarded by "If bfd.SessionState is not Down" and so does
# nothing at all here — not even the LocalDiag write — and §6.8.4 arms
# the timer only when the state is Init or Up. So all three are
# genuinely no-ops, and this arc is drawn to say that they are DEFINED
# no-ops rather than gaps. This is the one place the summary diagram
# and the normative text read differently.

transition init -[rx Down]-> init class=hold
# §6.8.6's Init branch handles received Init and Up only; received Down
# falls through, so the session stays in Init. The RFC diagram draws
# this self-loop and labels it "DOWN".

transition up -[rx Init, rx Up]-> up class=hold
# §6.8.6's Up branch handles received Down only; Init and Up fall
# through. The RFC diagram draws this self-loop and labels it
# "INIT, UP".

transition admindown -[any packet: discarded]-> admindown class=hold
# §6.8.6, first clause after the header checks: "If bfd.SessionState is
# AdminDown / Discard the packet". Every received Sta value, and the
# Detection Timer too (§6.8.4 fires only in Init or Up). The RFC's
# diagram cannot show this — it deletes AdminDown entirely — so this
# arc is present here and absent there, deliberately.

# ── RENDER DEFECTS PRESENT IN THE PUBLISHED ARTIFACT ────────────────
# Recorded rather than papered over. These are engine render defects,
# not limits of the language, and the figure ships with them visible:
#
#  1. SELF-LOOP LABEL STRUCK BY ITS OWN LOOP. The trigger label of a
#     self-transition is centred on the loop's outer stroke, so the loop
#     draws through the text. Visible on INIT ("rx Down"), on UP
#     ("rx Init, rx Up") and on ADMINDOWN ("any packet: discarded").
#     The DOWN self-loop escapes it only because its label is two lines.
#  2. UP's self-loop label is struck a SECOND time, by the long
#     Up → Down teardown edge running horizontally through it.
#  3. The three back edges into DOWN (from INIT, from UP, from
#     ADMINDOWN) route out to a side channel far to the right, so their
#     labels sit a long way from the states they connect. Correct, and
#     hard to follow.
#  4. Crowding at INIT: the "rx Init" label of Down → Up lands against
#     INIT's box edge and immediately above INIT's own self-loop label.
#
# Nothing below this line changes any of that. The usual fix — pin the
# states — was unavailable when these four were recorded; 0.4
# made it available (see the `flow` note above), and this artifact has
# not yet been re-made under it, so the four defects stand as measured.

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# COMPANION TABLE — the totality the statechart cannot assert
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Sections are separate documents, so this one declares the version its
# own surface needs: a table needs nothing from 0.2.
#
# Read this as the structural claim the figure above cannot make: every
# cell is filled, therefore §6.8.6 leaves no pair undefined. Twenty
# cells; nine of them are state changes and eleven are defined no-ops.
# Delete this section and the machine above is unchanged — and the
# assertion "nothing is missing" is gone with it.
figdown 0.1 table
table transitions "RFC 5880 §6.8.6 transition function — every (state, event) pair has a defined outcome"
| bfd.SessionState | rx Sta=0 AdminDown | rx Sta=1 Down | rx Sta=2 Init | rx Sta=3 Up | Detect Time expired (§6.8.4) |
|------------------|--------------------|---------------|---------------|-------------|------------------------------|
| DOWN (1)         | stay DOWN<br>(guarded: no diag) | → INIT | → UP | stay DOWN | stay DOWN<br>(timer runs only in Init/Up) |
| INIT (2)         | → DOWN<br>diag 3 | stay INIT | → UP | → UP | → DOWN<br>diag 1 |
| UP (3)           | → DOWN<br>diag 3 | → DOWN<br>diag 3 | stay UP | stay UP | → DOWN<br>diag 1 |
| ADMINDOWN (0)    | discard | discard | discard | discard | stay ADMINDOWN |

figdown 0.1 table
table admin "Administrative control (RFC 5880 §6.8.16) — outside the packet-reception rules"
| Action           | From                  | To         | Also                                             |
|------------------|-----------------------|------------|--------------------------------------------------|
| enable session   | ADMINDOWN             | DOWN       | the only exit from ADMINDOWN                     |
| disable session  | DOWN, INIT, UP        | ADMINDOWN  | set LocalDiag; cease sending BFD Echo packets     |
